## Recommended: Use `System.IO.Abstractions`

For `File.WriteAllText`, `File.ReadAllText`, and `Directory.Exists`, the best approach is the battle-tested **System.IO.Abstractions** NuGet package — no custom wrappers needed.

### 1. Install packages

```bash
dotnet add <YourProject> package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

### 2. Register in DI

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

### 3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes

```csharp
public class ConfigService(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string Load(string path)
        => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void Save(string path, string content)
        => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool DirectoryExists(string path)
        => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

### 4. Test with `MockFileSystem`

```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/data/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") },
});
// Pre-create directories as needed
mockFs.AddDirectory("/output");

var service = new ConfigService(mockFs);

Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", service.Load("/data/config.json"));
Assert.True(service.DirectoryExists("/output"));

service.Save("/output/result.txt", "hello");
Assert.Equal("hello", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/output/result.txt"));
```

---

**Why this over a custom wrapper?** `System.IO.Abstractions` covers the entire `System.IO` surface, so you won't need to extend it as you use more file APIs. The `MockFileSystem` gives you an in-memory filesystem out of the box — no mocking framework required.

Once you've added the package and updated DI, you can use the `migrate-static-to-wrapper` skill (just ask) to bulk-replace existing `File.*` / `Directory.*` call sites across your project.
